{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfixxuV76AVm9GoabBeFj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate `position_weighted_switch_impression`, and the self-check passed. It printed `OK`, with a finite ELPD-LOO of −2100.6, but that comes from a 100-draw, single-chain smoke fit. It is not comparable to the incumbent's −2105.0 from its full fit.\n\n**Hypothesis:** this keeps the current best model, `second_order_chance_motif_suspicion`, and makes one change. People read a sequence left to right, and their sense of how often it switches depends on where the switches are. Each transition gets a weight of exp(λ·position), where position runs from −0.5 for the first transition to +0.5 for the last. The weights are rescaled so they add up to the number of transitions. The fitted λ (prior Normal(0, 1.5)) picks the direction: above zero, the end of the sequence counts more (recency); below zero, the start counts more (primacy). At λ = 0 the model is exactly the incumbent. This uses where things happen in a sequence, which every current model ignores, because they all work from position-blind counts. Two sequences with the same number of switches, but with their streak in different places, now look differently random.\n\n**What the check flagged (warnings only, it still passed):**\n- **Pareto k:** 8.2% of trials have Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. Admission may therefore treat this model's ELPD-LOO as untrustworthy. I didn't check whether the incumbent's fit has the same problem, though its 400 per-person parameters are the likely cause and this model keeps them.\n- **R-hat:** convergence wasn't checked, because a single-chain fit can't report it.\n\n**Novelty risk:** admission rejects a model that predicts within 0.002 of an existing one. If the full fit puts λ near zero, this one would be rejected as a near-duplicate of the incumbent.\n\n**Not addressed:** the critique's response-si